WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.570
NARRATOR: Harold
Uris was a chemist.

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:08.608
Like Gerard Kuiper, he also had
to fight his way into science.

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:12.879
Uris' family was poor,
like Kuiper's, so he

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:16.182
took a job teaching grammar
school in a mining camp

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:19.018
in Montana.

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:20.487
The parents of one
of his students

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.655
urged him to find a
way to get to college.

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:24.858
[orchestral music]

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:28.962
Harold Uris took that advice
all the way to a Nobel Prize

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:31.698
in chemistry.

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:36.536
By 1949, he was riding high,
a distinguished professor

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:40.373
at the University of
Chicago, then and now one

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.376
of the world's great
capitals of science.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.712
But when Uris read
his morning paper,

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:51.217
something began to curdle
inside him, a rising resentment.

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:53.386
First, a pang at a
fellow scientist's

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:55.188
heightened celebrity.

00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:57.557
Well, that was normal.

00:00:57.624 --> 00:01:01.928
Then he got to the park about
the origin of the planets.

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:04.230
He was offended
then an astronomer

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:06.900
was making pronouncements
about the chemical nature

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.668
of the solar system.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:12.038
That was his turf.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:13.373
[shouting]

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.809
Scientists are human.

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.310
We're primates.

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:21.981
We carry the same evolutionary
baggage as everyone else.

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:24.717
Kuiper and Uris
were two alpha males

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:29.622
who chose scientific argument
as their weapon of combat.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:33.259
And the two men fought
over a single hostage,

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:34.260
a young student.

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:43.203
When Carl Sagan was a kid, he
lived here in a small apartment

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:44.204
in Brooklyn.

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:51.578
[ticking]

00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.519
[honking]

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:05.558
In the mid-1940s,
he made this drawing

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:08.328
filled with
predictions that is now

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:10.930
in the US Library of Congress.

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:14.100
[orchestral music]

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:29.816
[whoosh]

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:34.821
MAN: Three, two, one, zero.

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:36.422
All engines running.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.223
Lift off.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.593
We have a lift off.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.696
NARRATOR: In an era where life
here was in the last seconds

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:46.900
of its four billion
year captivity on Earth,

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:52.906
he dreamed of going to the
planets, and even to the stars.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:56.042
But he didn't want to just
go in his imagination.

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:58.177
He wanted to really go.

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:02.148
He wanted to know what those
worlds were really like.

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:04.150
And he knew that
the only way to do

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:07.987
that was to become a scientist.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:09.889
The boy would come
under the wings

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:12.191
of the two warring giants.

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:16.596
As much as they hated each
other, he loved them both.

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:19.132
Together, the three of
them would tear down

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:21.634
the walls between the sciences.

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:24.237
And the boy would
tear down the tallest

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:29.576
wall, the one between
science and everyone else.

